<main class="cs26" id="main">
<section class="hero26" aria-labelledby="page-title"><div class="wrap"><div class="crumbs"><a href="/">CallTeam</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><a href="/case-studies/">Case studies</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span>IT disaster recovery</span></div><div class="hero-grid"><div><div class="kicker">IT services appointment-setting case study</div><h1 id="page-title">22 booked meetings.<br>Six months of disaster recovery outreach.</h1><p class="lead">An IT disaster-recovery provider needed conversations with people responsible for critical systems. The campaign explored the current environment, recovery priorities and whether an introductory discussion had a useful purpose.</p><div class="tags"><span class="tag">IT services</span><span class="tag">Disaster recovery</span><span class="tag">Technical qualification</span></div><div class="actions"><a href="#campaign-result" class="button">See the campaign result <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a><a class="button alt" href="https://calendly.com/hello-callteam/new-meeting">Book a Free 30-Minute Call <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div><aside class="ledger" aria-label="Reported campaign result"><div class="ledger-top"><span>CAMPAIGN RECORD</span><span>CLIENT CONFIDENTIAL</span></div><div class="ledger-number">22</div><div class="ledger-label">booked sales meetings</div><p>A conversation about recovery starts with the environment the buyer actually runs.</p><div class="ledger-footer"><div><strong>6 months</strong><span>first campaign period</span></div><div><strong>IT</strong><span>infrastructure and security buyers</span></div></div><div class="ledger-flow"><span>Current setup</span><span>Recovery priorities</span><span>Technical handoff</span></div></aside></div><div class="evidence-caption">Client identity withheld.</div><nav class="jumpbar" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#company">Company and buyers</a><a href="#approach">Campaign approach</a><a href="#campaign-result">Results</a><a href="#apply">Tools and resources</a><a href="#questions">Questions</a></nav></div></section>
<section id="company" aria-labelledby="company-heading"><div class="wrap intro-grid"><div><div class="kicker">Company and market</div><h2 id="company-heading">A recovery services provider, represented with technical context.</h2><p>The company sold IT disaster-recovery and resilience services to businesses that depended on critical systems remaining available or being restored. The outreach connected the offer to the buyer’s current infrastructure and recovery responsibilities.</p><p>The campaign included contacts across several business sectors, including manufacturing and supply-chain organizations. Recent work also expanded into contractor accounts. These were distinct account contexts, not one identical technical situation.</p></div><dl class="fact-list"><div><dt>Company type</dt><dd>IT disaster-recovery and resilience services provider</dd></div><div><dt>Target buyers</dt><dd>IT, infrastructure, security and relevant technology procurement leaders</dd></div><div><dt>Campaign window</dt><dd>First six months</dd></div><div><dt>Reported result</dt><dd>22 booked meetings</dd></div></dl></div></section>
<section class="tint" aria-labelledby="problem-heading"><div class="wrap"><div class="kicker">The technical sales problem</div><h2 id="problem-heading">“We have backups” left important questions open.</h2><p class="section-intro">A useful recovery discussion needed more than a generic security pitch. The caller had to establish the right owner and enough context for a relevant technical conversation.</p><div class="three"><article class="detail-card"><span class="card-no">01</span><h3>Ownership</h3><p>Was the contact responsible for infrastructure or recovery, able to evaluate it, or able to route the discussion?</p></article><article class="detail-card"><span class="card-no">02</span><h3>Current environment</h3><p>What systems and arrangements did the buyer already have, and what was still unknown?</p></article><article class="detail-card"><span class="card-no">03</span><h3>Meeting purpose</h3><p>Was there a recovery priority worth exploring with the provider, or only an early request for information?</p></article></div></div></section>
<section id="approach" aria-labelledby="message-heading"><div class="wrap"><div class="kicker">The conversation strategy</div><h2 id="message-heading">Give the buyer a specific question to answer.</h2><div class="message-grid"><div class="message-old"><span class="label">The broad pitch to avoid</span><p>A broad claim about better cybersecurity and business protection.</p></div><div class="message-new"><span class="label">The campaign’s practical direction</span><blockquote>How are you keeping critical systems available, and what would be useful to review about your recovery setup?</blockquote></div></div><p class="caption">Adapted from the campaign’s recovery-readiness approach. Technical details were established through follow-up rather than assumed from a job title.</p><div style="margin-top:48px"><div class="kicker">Execution decisions</div><h2>Find the owner before trying to fill the calendar.</h2><ol class="steps"><li><h3>Research the account</h3><p>Identify a relevant organization and the roles likely to own infrastructure, recovery or security decisions.</p></li><li><h3>Open the recovery question</h3><p>Discuss the current setup and the buyer’s responsibilities before introducing a list of capabilities.</p></li><li><h3>Follow up to qualify</h3><p>Gather missing technical context, reconnect with earlier interest and distinguish curiosity from a meeting-ready discussion.</p></li><li><h3>Hand off the environment</h3><p>Give the provider the buyer’s role, known setup, open questions, priorities and agreed meeting purpose.</p></li></ol></div><aside class="handoff" aria-label="Useful sales handoff fields"><div><span class="kicker">Qualification and handoff</span><h3>Help the technical team begin in the right place.</h3><p>The first meeting is more useful when the provider can see what is confirmed and what still needs discovery.</p></div><dl><div><dt>Responsible role</dt><dd>Ownership of infrastructure, security, recovery or the evaluation path</dd></div><div><dt>Current environment</dt><dd>Systems and recovery arrangements the buyer has actually confirmed</dd></div><div><dt>Discussion priority</dt><dd>The recovery question or operational concern worth exploring</dd></div><div><dt>Open items</dt><dd>Technical information, participants and next actions still to establish</dd></div></dl></aside></div></section>
<section id="campaign-result" class="tint" aria-labelledby="result-heading"><div class="wrap result-grid"><div><div class="kicker">The campaign result</div><h2 id="result-heading">Twenty-two booked meetings over six months.</h2><p>Over its first six months, the campaign booked 22 meetings for IT disaster-recovery and resilience services.</p><p>Live calling and continued follow-up kept the conversation grounded in the buyer’s current environment, recovery responsibilities and technical questions.</p><div class="measure-note"><p>The 22 bookings cover the mixed-sector campaign. Attendance and downstream sales figures were not reported.</p></div></div><aside class="result-panel" aria-label="Campaign result figures"><div class="kicker">One campaign. Its actual period.</div><div class="result-row"><strong>22</strong><span>booked meetings</span></div><div class="result-row"><strong>6</strong><span>campaign months</span></div><p class="result-note">Results cover this campaign and period. Individual campaign outcomes vary.</p></aside></div></section>
<section id="apply" aria-labelledby="apply-heading"><div class="wrap"><div class="kicker">Apply the campaign lesson</div><h2 id="apply-heading">Put the evidence to work in your own campaign.</h2><div class="tool-panel"><div><div class="kicker">Free tool • Meeting Quality Check</div><h3>Separate booked appointments from opportunity progress.</h3><p>Check attendance, sales acceptance and opportunity creation using one group of bookings. That gives your technical sales team a clearer view of the next question to investigate.</p></div><a class="button" href="/b2b-meeting-quality-check/">Check Your Meeting Results <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="two-tools"><article class="mini-tool"><div class="kicker">Plan the conversation</div><h3>Build your campaign preview.</h3><p>Choose your offer and buyer. Explore a starting call angle, qualification questions and related resources.</p><a class="text-link" href="/b2b-outbound-campaign-planner/">Build My Campaign Preview <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="mini-tool"><div class="kicker">Explore the economics</div><h3>Use your own numbers.</h3><p>Model costs, conversion assumptions and customer value. Keep the planning scenario separate from the historical result above.</p><a class="text-link" href="/b2b-lead-generation-roi-calculator/">Open the ROI Calculator <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></div></div></section>
<section class="tint" aria-labelledby="resource-heading"><div class="wrap"><div class="kicker">The connected resource path</div><h2 id="resource-heading">Go deeper on the market, buyer and execution.</h2><div class="three"><article class="resource-card"><span class="kicker">Industry playbook</span><h3>IT services outbound</h3><p>Plan account selection, technical discovery and useful meetings for a defined IT offer.</p><a class="text-link" href="/articles/it-services-outbound-sales-playbook/">Read the IT services playbook <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="resource-card"><span class="kicker">Buyer playbook</span><h3>Reach security leaders with context</h3><p>Understand the risk, ownership and evidence a security buyer needs before continuing a vendor discussion.</p><a class="text-link" href="/articles/ciso-outbound-sales-playbook/">Explore the CISO playbook <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article><article class="resource-card"><span class="kicker">Managed execution</span><h3>Outsourced SDR services</h3><p>Connect account research, calling, follow-up, qualification and sales-ready context in one managed engagement.</p><a class="text-link" href="/services/outsourced-sdr/">Explore outsourced SDR services <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></article></div><p class="caption">Working on your own opener? <a class="text-link" href="/cold-call-script-checker/">Check Your Cold Call Script <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>. Planning the engagement? <a class="text-link" href="/b2b-cold-calling-campaign-process/">See Your First 60 Days With CallTeam <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>.</p></div></section>
<section id="questions" aria-labelledby="faq-heading"><div class="wrap faq-layout"><div><div class="kicker">Campaign questions</div><h2 id="faq-heading">The company, the work and what the figures mean.</h2><p>Clear definitions make the case easier to evaluate and the lessons easier to use.</p></div><div><details><summary>What kind of IT company was represented?</summary><p>An IT disaster-recovery and resilience services provider. The campaign concerned the buyer’s critical systems, current recovery setup and the reason for a technical discussion.</p></details><details><summary>What was the campaign result?</summary><p>The practitioner reported 22 booked meetings during the first six months. This study does not report held meetings, proposals, customer wins or closed revenue.</p></details><details><summary>Was this a manufacturing-only campaign?</summary><p>No. The campaign included several business sectors and account lists. The 22 bookings are reported as a campaign total, not as a manufacturing-only or contractor-only result.</p></details><details><summary>Why qualify the environment before booking?</summary><p>The provider needs enough context to know why the conversation is useful and who should attend. A relevant title or a request for information does not establish the current technical setup.</p></details><details><summary>Does a meeting prove the buyer needs a new recovery provider?</summary><p>No. The meeting establishes a reason to explore the buyer’s question. Requirements, fit, evaluation and any change decision still need to be established.</p></details></div></div></section>
